Installation & Environment Setup

1) Install Python
Check installation
python --version

2) Create Virtual Environment 
python -m venv venv

Activate:
Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

3) Install Django
pip install django

Create a New Django Project
django-admin startproject project_name
cd project_name

Run Development Server
python manage.py runserver
Open in browser: http://127.0.0.1:8000/

Create a Django App
python manage.py startapp app_name

Register the app in settings.py:
INSTALLED_APPS = [
    'app_name',
]
After created:
to use or create anything create a file name "templates" inside app then you that to create html,python ,etc..

once created create a fuction inside view like def home(request):
                                                   return render("hello")

then go to the url and configure it by first import-ing it like 'from django.view import . or home'
then below path('home','home',name="home_url")
then you go to home page and call it


A Model in Django is a Python class that defines:

The structure of your database table

The fields (columns)

The relationships between tables

Every model you create becomes a table in the database.
- CharField → Short text
- TextField → Long text
- IntegerField → Whole numbers
- FloatField → Decimal numbers
- BooleanField → True/False
- DateTimeField → Date & time
- EmailField → Email
- ImageField → Images
- FileField → Files

eg:
from django.db import models

class Product(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    quantity = models.IntegerField()
    price = models.FloatField()
    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.name

there are relationship like one to one, one to many, many to many 

ADMIN PANEL
Create admin user:
python manage.py createsuperuser

After creating or changing models:
python manage.py makemigrations ->recored the changes
python manage.py migrate-> save the changes


github part
VS Code → File → Open Folder → select project

Initialize Git
terminal:git init

Commit Your Files
Click + (Stage All)
Type message: Initial commit or first commit
Click Commit

Create Repo on GitHub
Copy the repo URL.

Connect to GitHub

In VS Code terminal:
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repo-name.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

After changes
git add .
git commit -m "your message"
git push

